# rm515, the straw price tag (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm515
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_hakon_grantham
written_by codex
title the straw price tag
fits stage=young|adolescent|adult|old
prefers drive=restless honesty=plain
traits temper=nervy talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=restless
quirk ties_straw_price_tags_to_pretend_ships
likes barley_soup old_tales fresh_straw summer_evenings
dislikes waiting drizzle
fears a_boat_sold_before_its_name_is_read a_stall_with_nobody_at_it
hopes a_small_market_stall_under_an_awning_of_its_own
topics smalltalk +0.6 travel +0.5 home +0.3 food +0.3
styles comfort=gentle gossip=plain refuse=quick tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# twists straw into ship tags, counts imaginary customers twice and keeps careful count of the real coins saved towards a stall.

words
oath! = Split my little straw flag | A hole in the penny bag
saying! = Name the boat before pricing the berth | Two straws make a decent mast
strawtag = a loop of yellow straw | my straw ticket
coinwell = a hollow for one penny | my little penny hollow
mast = a tall straw stem | a straw with a bent tip
soupbit = a round grain of barley | a fat little soup grain
storyend = the end of an old yarn | the last turn of the tale
floorstraw = fresh crackly straw | a clean scatter of straw
rainline = a fine line of drizzle | a tickle of little drops
slate = my narrow tally slate | a little square of slate
fleetbit = a boat shaped like a slipper | the one with a high bow
goldedge = the gold rim on a cloud | the last gold on the roof
clothstall = a folded square of cloth | my miniature awning

any
opinion @1 city for 1 : There's a harbour for spotting boats and a market for finding out what they bring in.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : A good seller waits long enough for a small customer to sort the pennies properly.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : A large house makes a splendid pretend shop up to the moment everything needs carrying upstairs.
opinion @1 road for 1 : My favourite stretch of road comes out by the market.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : The feast stalls bring out strange little goods and put them all on show.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New faces see things in my little ships that nobody at home has spotted.
opinion @1 times mixed 0 : A lot can happen while you're waiting to find out what your first penny will buy.
line @1 greet : Come see the boat with the straw price.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : A visitor I haven't counted yet.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. That mast has a gold tip.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening makes the little fleet look nearly real.
line @1 greet.return : I heard you through all my counting.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : I know where you fit in the row.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : My fingers can finish this twist on their own.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Even in the dark I remember which tag is yours.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Come and have the corner berth, kin.
line @1 again.open : The last penny hasn't moved away.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Friend, be the first customer again!
line @1 farewell : Go home with a good wind in your sleeves.
line @1 farewell.return : And a dry corner for the pennies.
line @1 react.news : Tell it where I can hear each part.
line @1 react.news.new : That's another ship for the first-time list.
line @1 react.news.known : I could count that one with my eyes shut.
line @1 react.news.doubt : I want a closer look at the odd bit.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : I'll move the little boats aside so you can tell it quietly.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : That gives my biggest straw flag something to do.
line @1 ask.how : Did something good arrive for you?
line @1 answer.how : I've still got the important penny.
line @1 thank : You spotted the dropped price tag. Thank you.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It wasn't a bother to shift a straw.
line @1 sympathise : I'll keep a place for you while you rest.
line @1 reassure : We can count again if we need to.
line @1 decline : I've already put my good straw to another use.
line @1 accept_decline : All right. Nobody has to buy a boat.
line @1 preclose : I'd better tie up the fleet before supper.
line @1 accept_preclose : Best take that boat before the price goes up.
scene gather_news
when topic=news world.news
does tell.news / react.self / assess
a : #world.news#. The tag on that word is #world.news_from#. | #world.news_from.capitalize# is where this came from. #world.news#.
b : Would you move the tag? | Shall we count it twice?
a : No. I'd like the next listener to see the tag on the original account. | Once is plenty till there's something more to add.

scene gossip_buy
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.gossip / react.self / assess
a : A bit of chatter's trying to sell itself as a whole story. | Some words arrive with a fancier flag than the ship underneath.
b : Would you pay much? | Is that one of those?
a : I'd keep the penny. | I didn't see enough hull for my liking.

story straw_soup [topic=memories|food]
  : I once balanced a straw ship across the lip of a soup bowl to show how big a harbour ought to be.
  : The straw bent in the steam and the bow went straight down.
  b : Was the crew lost?
  : The crew consisted entirely of a grain of barley. I sent my spoon down for the rescue.

story price_mark [topic=memories|work]
  : I chalked a fine large price on a scrap board and leant it against a model.
  : Standing opposite the board, I hardly recognised my own figure.
  b : What had you charged?
  : One upside-down hook. I'd never considered the view a customer gets until then.

story sail_case [topic=memories|travel]
  : I put my favourite bit of straw in a box and carried it about for luck.
  : Opened the lid to show a visitor and found a very ordinary stem.
  b : Had you taken the wrong one?
  : I turned the box round. It had merely sailed to the other end and faced the opposite way.

rule @count_tags
when concept=bark here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 8h
say One dry flag for each little hull.

rule @end_tale
when concept=bark here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Plenty of evening for the last boat.

rule @drip_wrap
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Turn the prices under cover.

rule @straw_clear
when concept=bark here.place=street
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Mind the low straw mast.

dlg @1 resident.hub : Look from this end. The little flag points towards you.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, you can have your old place by the awning.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=dusk] : Evening. The fleet still has its bright edges.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Leave my tags where they are and I'll listen from here.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=child] : I've lessons to attend to. Putting prices on my pretend ships makes the numbers more interesting.
dlg @2 resident.job : I mind my figures and try to keep the prices clear enough to read.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : On this word the place is #world.news_from#. #world.news#.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Somebody's made that story a wonderfully tall mast. I'd sooner inspect the hull.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Watch for a little gold sail on your way.
graft resident.hub
choice How do you write a price on straw? -> bend_price
node bend_price
say A bend for one penny and a nick across it for another. I wouldn't ask a stranger to guess. I say the price very clearly too.
choice What happens when prices get bigger? -> many_straws
choice I can see the bend. -> hub
node many_straws
say I'll need a chalk board then. That's one of the things the savings are meant for. A stall with no prices might as well be a collection of my favourite things.
choice A chalk board sounds useful. -> hub
choice I'll leave your pennies in their hollow. -> bye


grown
scene look_again
when topic=travel here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can still pick a ship out across the Sound by the line of its bow. | The little fleets of straw have a way of coming back to mind at a busy harbour.
b : What else do you see now? | Does one help with the other?
a : The long day's work that will come off by the sling once it's moored. | It taught me to notice a small difference in two similar outlines.

scene ticket_back
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A straw price tag could be changed merely by turning its bent end over. | I once believed owning the counter was the great part of keeping a stall.
b : Handy for a seller? | And now?
a : Handy for a child. In grown company I name the price before the goods move. | I'd want the custom that keeps a counter worth opening.

scene hands_quiet
when topic=home self.quarter=heights
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A Heights window can put an entire street's activity into a quiet frame. | A quiet landing in the Heights has room for an imaginary stall.
b : Is that enough bustle? | Do you still set it up?
a : Often I prefer to go down to it. But I like choosing where to begin. | In my head occasionally. Even the rent there is quite plausible.

scene shared_story
when topic=memories self.likes=old_tales
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I enjoy hearing a familiar tale open slowly enough to include a new listener. | My favourite ship stories are the ones with a price to pay on landing.
b : Even if everybody else knows? | A fee?
a : Especially then. It's nice to hear a familiar ending reach one fresh pair of ears. | Sometimes just a promise to come home in time.

scene trade_space
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A space of my own in the market remains a pleasing prospect. | My ideal awning keeps two heads dry in a shower.
b : What would make it yours? | Why just two?
a : I could decide for myself which price goes at a height a young customer can read. | The seller and the customer are a sensible place to start.


young
scene name_hull
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : This one's #fleetbit#. I know the real one by its nose. | I give each ship #strawtag#.
b : Can you know a boat's nose? | What does the tag say?
a : Yes. Even when the rest of its face is round the corner. | Where I ought to put it back. I can read a bent straw.

scene single_penny
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My stall pennies fit inside #coinwell#. | One day my shop will have #clothstall# over it.
b : Is there room for a whole stall? | What if it rains?
a : The stall doesn't go inside. I only need to fit the money. | I shall put my own head under the best part.

scene lessons_line
when topic=work self.job=child
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can make a counting lesson out of five ships arriving. | I put #slate# down beside my pretend stall.
b : What if one sails away? | For the sums?
a : Then I have a taking-away lesson as well. | So my customers can't say I charged twice. I look very carefully.

scene fresh_bed
when topic=home self.likes=fresh_straw
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #floorstraw.capitalize# makes a splendid market when nobody's stepped on it yet. | I pick my mast out before the straw goes down.
b : Where are the stalls? | Why before?
a : Anywhere I can draw a square without breaking a stem. | After that a mast may have had a boot directly on its head.

scene soup_boats
when topic=food self.likes=barley_soup
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #soupbit.capitalize# has floated up beside my spoon. | I can wait for barley to cool if I blow on the biggest grain.
b : Is it a ship? | Why pick the biggest?
a : It was. I've just eaten it. | I'll certainly know when that one reaches the other bank.

scene tale_again
when topic=memories self.likes=old_tales
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like #storyend# even when I've heard it plenty. | Tell me a tale with a ship in it and I'll put the right one on my knee.
b : Why does it help to know the ending? | What if the ship sinks?
a : I get to wait for the exact good bit. | Then my knee will make an excellent island.

scene father_straw
when topic=family self.father
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like #self.father# to be my first proper customer. | I put my best ship where #self.father# can see it.
b : What would you sell? | What's the best part?
a : I'll decide once I've counted enough actual money for the stall. | Finding out if anybody can tell which one's best without being told.


adolescent
scene fair_pricing
when topic=work
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I used to give the fanciest hull the highest price. | The straw tag goes on the model till the figure's been agreed.
b : Has that changed? | Who takes it off?
a : Now I ask what the customer needs to carry. A pretty bow can be a nuisance to a crate. | Whoever has counted the payment. I'm learning not to rush that bit.

scene count_heads
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can recognise a real ship before I can count the customers along its side. | I used to call every tall mast important.
b : Which interests you more? | What catches your attention nowadays?
a : The ship first. Then I'm curious which cargo drew the crowd. | What the hull does when a load's lifted out of it.

scene warm_close
when topic=weather self.likes=summer_evenings
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #goldedge.capitalize# makes a fine signal to begin packing a practice stall. | In the hush of a summer evening I try calling a price out loud.
b : Why so early? | To whom?
a : I like finishing in daylight, with all my best scraps still in view. | To my own ears first. They spot a silly figure remarkably quickly.

scene drizzle_tags
when topic=weather self.dislikes=drizzle
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #rainline.capitalize# can blur a price without washing the old one off. | Drizzle softens straw till the little bows all lose their shape.
b : Can you trace over it? | Do you still make those?
a : I'd start with a dry tag. Two prices on one card do very little for a seller's credit. | I like owning a fleet I can dry on a plate.

scene waiting_place
when topic=smalltalk self.dislikes=waiting
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Standing still by the market space is harder than carrying the board there. | Promise me noon and I'd take that over a whole morning of being nearly next.
b : What helps? | Even for good ground?
a : Counting how many buyers stop short because a stall has left no room. | Good ground will be there at noon. My morning will have gone.

scene open_counter
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I want a counter wide enough for two hands to meet across it. | Saving towards a stall takes longer now I know what the cover costs.
b : Just two? | Will you give up the cover?
a : One pays and one returns change. That's business enough to begin. | Never. I've read what drizzle can do to arithmetic.
